            <div xml:id="d599">

                <pb facs="0693" n="683" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg683"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.0093/552: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-09-15T23:00:00-04:00">September 15,
                            1942—11 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>837. Your 807, July 8, noon, and Department’s 639, July 16, 9 p.m.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d599-f1"/> The Department would appreciate receiving your views, as
                    requested in its telegram under reference, in regard to the question of the
                    possible opening of temporary offices at Lanchow and Chengtu, as well as at
                    Kweilin and Sian as recommended by you. Lanchow, for example, might be of
                    special importance as an observation post. An office at Chengtu would, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">inter alia</hi>, serve the interests of a considerable
                    number of Americans.</p>
                <p>It is suggested that, after study of the matter, and unless you <pb facs="0694"
                        n="684" xml:id="pg684"/> perceive objection, you approach the Foreign Office
                    informally with a view to obtaining its consent to the opening of such of the
                    suggested temporary offices as seem to you advisable, not of course as a matter
                    of right, but purely as a matter of reciprocity and as in the interests of both
                    countries. If the Chinese Government should prefer that any or all of the
                    suggested new offices be considered and listed as merely temporary branch
                    offices of the consular section of the Embassy or of the Consulate at Kunming,
                    this Government would of course be disposed to meet Chinese wishes in this
                    respect.</p>
                <p>The Department would also appreciate your opinion as to the advisability of
                    raising the Consulate at Kunming to a Consulate General.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
